# 📐 Math for AI-SDET: Complete Course Overview

> **Your Path to Mastering Mathematics for LangChain, LangGraph, MLOps, MCP Servers & GenAI Testing**

---

## 🎯 Course Philosophy

**Target Audience:** AI-SDET • Automation Engineer • Backend/Agent Engineer • Beginner–Intermediate

**Teaching Approach:**
- ✅ Math from a **programming & engineering perspective**
- ✅ **Python-style thinking** everywhere
- ✅ Real-world **AI/Agent applications**
- ✅ **Mathematical foundations only** — practical application covered in Prompt Engineering course
- ❌ No exam-style theory
- ❌ No heavy proofs or derivations

**Final Goal:** Confidently understand the mathematical principles behind AI systems — from LangGraph agents to RAG pipelines.

---

## 🔄 Course Relationship: Math vs Prompt Engineering

This course focuses on **WHY** (mathematical foundations), while the **Prompt Engineering Course** focuses on **HOW** (practical techniques).

| This Course (Math for AI-SDET) | Prompt Engineering Course |
|-------------------------------|---------------------------|
| Why embeddings work mathematically | How to use embeddings in RAG prompts |
| Entropy theory behind temperature | How to tune temperature for tasks |
| Graph theory for workflows | How to build LangGraph agents |
| Information theory concepts | Prompt optimization techniques |
| Mathematical optimization | Cost management strategies |

**Recommendation:** Complete this course first for solid foundations, then take Prompt Engineering for hands-on application.

---

## 📊 Course Structure at a Glance

| Phase | Focus Area | Modules | Key AI/SDET Applications |
|-------|------------|---------|--------------------------| 
| **00** | Thinking Like a Programmer | 4 | Problem decomposition, algorithmic thinking |
| **01** | Arithmetic Mastery | 5 | Precision, floating point, test assertions |
| **02** | Ratios & Percentages | 4 | Accuracy metrics, performance benchmarks |
| **03** | Algebra | 5 | Variables, equations, threshold logic |
| **04** | Spatial Thinking | 3 | Coordinates, vectors, distance calculations |
| **05** | Sets & Logic | 3 | Boolean conditions, test coverage, data filtering |
| **06** | Statistics | 3 | Model evaluation, data analysis |
| **07** | Probability | 1 | Confidence scores, sampling, uncertainty |
| **08** | Engineering Math | 1 | Scaling, complexity, growth patterns |
| **09** | CS Math Essentials | 1 | Modular arithmetic, recursion, matrices, hashing, bits |
| **10** | Agent & LLM Math | 5 | Graphs, probability, embeddings, entropy, training theory |

**Total: 35+ Modules across 11 Phases**

---

## 📚 Detailed Phase Breakdown

---

### 🧠 Phase 00: Thinking Like a Programmer
*Foundation for mathematical problem-solving in code*

| Module | Topic | Key Concepts |
|--------|-------|--------------|
| 01 | Number Fundamentals | Types, representation, binary/hex |
| 02 | Comparison Logic | Equality, ordering, comparison operators |
| 03 | Number Properties | Even/odd, divisibility, prime concepts |
| 04 | Basic Operations | Arithmetic in programming context |

**AI Application:** Building intuition for how data is represented and compared in AI systems.

---

### ➕ Phase 01: Arithmetic Mastery
*Precision and calculation skills for testing*

| Module | Topic | Key Concepts |
|--------|-------|--------------|
| 05 | Advanced Operations | Power, roots, order of operations |
| 06 | Number Relationships | Factors, multiples, GCD/LCM |
| 07 | Fractional Numbers | Fractions in computation |
| 08 | Decimal System | Decimal precision, base conversion |
| 09 | Precision & Accuracy | Floating point, rounding errors, tolerance |

**AI Application:** Understanding why `0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3` matters in test assertions and model evaluation.

---

### 📊 Phase 02: Ratios & Percentages
*Performance metrics and benchmarking*

| Module | Topic | Key Concepts |
|--------|-------|--------------|
| 10 | Ratios | Part-to-part relationships, scaling |
| 11 | Percentages | Percentage calculations, change rates |
| 12 | Proportional Reasoning | Cross-multiplication, scaling |
| 13 | Time, Distance, Speed | Rate problems, performance metrics |

**AI Application:** Accuracy, precision, recall calculations; latency benchmarking; throughput analysis.

---

### 🔢 Phase 03: Algebra
*Variables, equations, and threshold logic*

| Module | Topic | Key Concepts |
|--------|-------|--------------|
| 14 | Variables & Expressions | Symbolic representation, evaluation |
| 15 | Simple Equations | Solving for unknowns |
| 16 | Substitution | Value replacement, parameterization |
| 17 | Inequalities | Comparisons, threshold conditions |
| 18 | Absolute Values | Distance, error margins |

**AI Application:** Confidence thresholds, parameter tuning, constraint validation in agents.

---

### 📍 Phase 04: Spatial Thinking
*Coordinates, vectors, and distance*

| Module | Topic | Key Concepts |
|--------|-------|--------------|
| 19 | Coordinate Systems | 2D/3D positioning, axes |
| 20 | Distance Calculations | Euclidean distance, Manhattan distance, Minkowski |
| 21 | Basic Geometry | Shapes, areas, spatial relationships |

**AI Application:** Embedding spaces, vector similarity foundations, spatial representations in ML.

---

### 🔵 Phase 05: Sets & Logic
*Boolean reasoning and data operations*

| Module | Topic | Key Concepts |
|--------|-------|--------------|
| 22 | Set Theory | Sets, membership, Venn diagrams |
| 23 | Set Operations | Union, intersection, difference |
| 24-27 | Boolean Logic & Truth Tables | AND, OR, NOT, XOR, implications |

**AI Application:** Test coverage analysis, data filtering, conditional agent logic, query construction.

---

### 📈 Phase 06: Statistics
*Data analysis and model evaluation*

| Module | Topic | Key Concepts |
|--------|-------|--------------|
| 28-29 | Central Tendency | Mean, median, mode |
| 30-31 | Spread & Standard Deviation | Variance, distribution spread |
| 32-34 | Data Analysis & Distribution | Histograms, normal distribution, outliers |

**AI Application:** Model performance metrics, A/B testing, quality monitoring, anomaly detection.

---

### 🎲 Phase 07: Probability
*Uncertainty and confidence*

| Module | Topic | Key Concepts |
|--------|-------|--------------|
| 35-40 | Probability Fundamentals | Basic probability, events, expected value |
|        | Conditional Probability | P(A\|B), Bayes' theorem basics |
|        | Distributions | Binomial, normal distribution intuition |

**AI Application:** LLM confidence scores, sampling strategies, uncertainty quantification.

---

### ⚙️ Phase 08: Engineering Math
*Scaling and complexity*

| Module | Topic | Key Concepts |
|--------|-------|--------------|
| 41-46 | Engineering Math | Logarithms, exponentials, Big-O notation |
|        | Growth Patterns | Linear vs exponential scaling |
|        | Complexity Analysis | Time/space complexity for AI pipelines |

**AI Application:** Token cost scaling, batch size optimization, pipeline efficiency.

---

### 💻 Phase 09: CS Math Essentials
*Core mathematical concepts for building robust software systems*

| Module | Topic | Key Concepts |
|--------|-------|--------------|
| 06 | CS Math Essentials | Modular arithmetic, recursion, matrices, hashing, bit manipulation |

**AI Application:** Understanding the low-level data structures and algorithmic complexity of AI memory and state management.

---

### 🤖 Phase 10: Agent & LLM Math (REFINED)
*Pure mathematical foundations for modern AI systems*

> **Note:** This phase covers the **mathematical theory** only. Practical application techniques (RAG pipelines, caching strategies, agentic workflows, prompt optimization) are covered in the **Prompt Engineering Course**.

| Module | Topic | Key Mathematical Concepts | ⚠️ NOT Covered Here |
|--------|-------|--------------------------|---------------------|
| 01 | Graph Theory Basics | Nodes, edges, directed graphs, DAGs, adjacency matrices, topological sort, state machine theory | LangGraph implementation, workflow building |
| 02 | Conditional Probability for Agents | Bayes' theorem, prior/posterior probability, confidence intervals, decision theory | Retry logic implementation, fallback strategies |
| 03 | Embeddings & Vector Space Theory | Vector spaces, dimensionality, cosine similarity formula, dot products, high-dimensional geometry | RAG pipeline building, similarity threshold tuning |
| 04 | Information Theory | Entropy, cross-entropy, surprisal, KL-divergence, mutual information, perplexity derivation | Temperature tuning, prompt optimization |
| 05 | LLM Training Theory | Cross-entropy loss, gradient descent intuition, RLHF math, DPO formulation, perplexity as evaluation | Fine-tuning workflows, training data curation |

**What Changed:**
- ❌ **Removed:** Module 04 (Optimization Thinking) → Moved to Prompt Engineering (cost/caching is practical, not math)
- ❌ **Removed:** Module 06 (RAG and DAG Architectures) → Merged with Module 01 (graph theory) and Module 03 (embeddings)
- ✅ **Retained:** Pure mathematical concepts that underpin AI systems
- ✅ **Renamed:** Module 05 from "Information Theory Basics" to "Information Theory" (keeping full coverage)
- ✅ **Renamed:** Module 07 to Module 05 and renamed to "LLM Training Theory"

**AI Application:** This phase provides the **mathematical intuition** for understanding why AI systems work — essential for debugging, optimization, and advanced system design.

---

## 🔗 Phase 10 → Prompt Engineering Course Mapping

| Math for AI-SDET (This Course) | Prompt Engineering Course |
|-------------------------------|---------------------------|
| Module 01: Graph Theory | Module 7.2: Agentic Workflows (practical) |
| Module 02: Conditional Probability | Module 7.5: Error Handling (decision logic) |
| Module 03: Embeddings Theory | Module 7.4: RAG Integration (practical) |
| Module 04: Information Theory | Module 8.2: Caching + Module 8.3: Context (practical) |
| Module 05: LLM Training Theory | Module 5.5: Self-Refinement (application) |

---

## 🎯 Learning Outcomes

After completing this course, you will be able to:

### For AI Development
- ✅ **Understand LangGraph workflows** through graph theory foundations
- ✅ **Reason about RAG systems** with vector space mathematics
- ✅ **Analyze entropy and perplexity** for model evaluation
- ✅ **Apply probability theory** to confidence-based reasoning

### For AI Testing (SDET)
- ✅ **Test numerical precision** in AI outputs
- ✅ **Validate probability-based logic** in agents
- ✅ **Benchmark performance** with proper statistical methods
- ✅ **Design edge case tests** for ML systems

### For Interviews
- ✅ **Explain mathematical concepts** in practical terms
- ✅ **Discuss the "why"** behind AI system design
- ✅ **Demonstrate deep understanding** of LLM internals

